{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And Rachel saw, etc.</b> There is a fine point to examine in its saying <b>to Jacob</b>, for he was proven not to be sterile, and in this matter there was no cause to attribute it to him but only to her. And it says <b>and she was jealous of her sister</b> — meaning, because she was her sister, and presumably she would resemble her in temperament and nature, yet this one gave birth and that one did not. For if she were not her sister, she might have said that the reason she did not give birth was that their temperaments were not matched, and the like." "<b>Further,</b> it intends in its saying <b>to Jacob</b>, that the core of her distress was the very fact that he was Jacob, for if it had been another man who was not renowned for his righteousness, she would have attributed it to him, that the reason was (Psalms 125:3) ״כִּי לֹא יָנוּחַ שֵׁבֶט הָרֶשַׁע עַל גּוֹרַל הַצַּדִּיקִים״ — that she herself was righteous, and this was the cause preventing pregnancy. But since Jacob was an unmistakably proven righteous man, therefore she was jealous of her sister who merited this."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 30, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:44:59.842046553Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}